  "design": "51 states x T=120 months, cohorts (48,72,96) at 20% each, 40% never treated, Poisson recording map, outcome log((Y+0.5)/exposure); matches results/sims/e_calibrated",
  "caveats": [
    "The label-permutation test needs C! >= 1/alpha distinct permuted values. With C = 3 the two-sided Wald takes at most 3 distinct values over the 6 permutations (equally spaced d makes reversal a sign flip), so its attainable p-values are {1/3, 2/3, 1} and it CANNOT reject at 5%; the one-sided directed statistic attains 1/6 = 0.167, still above 5%. Size 0.000 at C = 3 in size_table.csv is that resolution bound, not a coding artefact. Use the cluster sign-flip or wild-bootstrap tests when C <= 4.",
    "Exactness of the label-permutation test requires exchangeability of the loading-normalised category arrays. The DGP satisfies it by construction (loadings are a uniformly random permutation of a fixed set, drawn independently of d, and the idiosyncratic errors are i.i.d. across categories). Cells with category-specific error variances or block-correlated categories are NOT simulated here; theory/inference.md Section 3.4 gives the asymptotic (approximate-symmetry) fallback.",
    "The wild cluster bootstrap is applied to the partialled-out regression (fixed effects removed once, weights applied to the residualised residuals) rather than re-absorbing the fixed effects on every draw. The difference is O_p(1/G) and standard in partialling-out implementations, but it is an implementation choice, not the literal CGM algorithm.",
    "Size adjustment uses the alpha-quantile of the p-value distribution from the matching kappa = 0 cell of the SAME configuration (no borrowing across designs). Size-adjusted power at kappa = 0 is 0.05 by construction.",
    "MDE inversion is linear interpolation on a coarse kappa grid with 500-600 replications per cell (MCSE <= 0.02), so simulated MDEs are accurate to roughly +/- 0.05; read the closed-form/simulation comparison as a check on the formula's shape and level, not to two decimals.",
    "Under the Poisson recording map the wedge is log1p(kappa d_c), not kappa d_c; the closed-form MDE column 'mde_wald' solves the nonlinear version and 'mde_wald_linear' the linearised one. At kappa ~ 0.5 the two differ by roughly 15-20%.",
    "sigma^2 is the delta^2-weighted mean of the category residual variances of the loading-normalised outcome, estimated on the kappa = 0 cells. In an application it would come from pilot data; it is the only quantity in the MDE formula that is not a pure design object."
  ],
